well tonight local doctors from the  clark county medical society  are gathering virtually to discuss the  rollout of the vaccine  medical professionals from a wide range  of specialties are going to be taking  part in that town hall  8 news now reporter kate houston spoke  to a local ob gyn who is taking part  about the impact the vaccine could have  on pregnant women  this week the american college of  obstetricians and gynecologists  recommended that the coven 19 vaccine  not be withheld from pregnant women  physicians say it comes down to what a  woman thinks is best  for her and her growing family local ob  gyn dr stacy mchale has no concerns  about the covid19 vaccine  she says while pregnant women are not  necessarily at a higher risk of  contracting the virus  they are at a higher risk of severe  complications if they do get it  pregnant women who are high risk for  contracting coven 19 should strongly  consider getting the vaccination while  pregnant  as with most other vaccinations dr  mchale says the recommendation is  different for women trying to conceive  women who are trying to get pregnant  have been advised to not receive the  vaccine in the two months before  pregnancy if at all possible  the american college of obstetricians  and gynecologists notes that pregnant  women were not included in the covet 19  vaccine trials while safety data is not  currently available  the acog adds that there is also no data  to indicate the vaccine should be  withheld  this is a new disease we don't have all  the information  you have to make the best choice with  your health care provider to determine  what the right course of acts are you  kate houston 8 news now
 

